WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.070 --> 00:00:06.300
I want to talk today about a more sort of
realistic and finely tuned possible roadmap

00:00:06.300 --> 00:00:11.959
for starting to reopen the economy, starting
to get people back to work, something that

00:00:11.959 --> 00:00:17.529
will get us more in the direction of what
we all felt was kind of normal life up until

00:00:17.529 --> 00:00:19.200
a few months ago.

00:00:19.200 --> 00:00:24.890
Donald Trump has been making very strong pronouncements
about he gets to decide he has absolute authority

00:00:24.890 --> 00:00:26.210
over governors.

00:00:26.210 --> 00:00:30.470
He's starting to provide lists of states that
are ready and lists of states that are not.

00:00:30.470 --> 00:00:32.660
And those that should do this and those that
should do that.

00:00:32.660 --> 00:00:34.170
And it's all happening.

00:00:34.170 --> 00:00:40.510
Absent a broader understanding of how the
economic questions relate to the health questions,

00:00:40.510 --> 00:00:46.430
relate to testing questions, relate to types
of tests and medical equipment and supplies.

00:00:46.430 --> 00:00:51.250
And so I thought we would go through something
that's a little bit more fact-based.

00:00:51.250 --> 00:00:56.980
And there's actually a very good roadmap done
by Morgan Stanley, the financial firm.

00:00:56.980 --> 00:00:57.980
I know.

00:00:57.980 --> 00:01:01.280
But it's a it's actually a great roadmap that
takes into consideration a lot of these different

00:01:01.280 --> 00:01:06.869
elements which we will be looking at and a
few important things that we sort of need

00:01:06.869 --> 00:01:11.040
to be thinking about that this roadmap does
think about.

00:01:11.040 --> 00:01:18.000
One is it considers when are the multiple
peaks of the U.S. likely to be because different

00:01:18.000 --> 00:01:21.860
parts of the U.S. are going to peak at different
points in time.

00:01:21.860 --> 00:01:27.540
And we believe we may be at the peak or plateau
or just past it nationally, but it is still

00:01:27.540 --> 00:01:29.909
very much regionalized.

00:01:29.909 --> 00:01:34.970
Number two, it takes into consideration testing
capacity and it also bears in mind the possibility

00:01:34.970 --> 00:01:37.619
or likelihood of a second wave of infection.

00:01:37.619 --> 00:01:40.290
And this is one of the things that's so important
to understand.

00:01:40.290 --> 00:01:43.030
If you're going to talk about people getting
back to work, you have to talk about testing

00:01:43.030 --> 00:01:47.710
capacity if you're going to have the conversation
about getting back to work.

00:01:47.710 --> 00:01:53.840
You need to be talking about not only the
RNA tests, but also antibody tests and more

00:01:53.840 --> 00:01:58.340
testing allows more people to go back to work
in two ways.

00:01:58.340 --> 00:02:03.970
Number one, you can figure out who has the
antibodies and therefore is a good candidate

00:02:03.970 --> 00:02:05.520
to go back to work.

00:02:05.520 --> 00:02:11.020
Number two, you can more quickly figure out
who is sick and you can immediately quarantine

00:02:11.020 --> 00:02:12.020
them.

00:02:12.020 --> 00:02:13.160
You've got to establish contact tracing.

00:02:13.160 --> 00:02:16.460
And you also have to make sure that the people
they've been in touch with are not out in

00:02:16.460 --> 00:02:17.460
public.

00:02:17.460 --> 00:02:19.190
So a lot of this stuff has to be done together.

00:02:19.190 --> 00:02:23.720
The amount of testing that needs to be done
is dramatically higher than what we have the

00:02:23.720 --> 00:02:25.050
capacity to do now.

00:02:25.050 --> 00:02:26.160
And it is expensive.

00:02:26.160 --> 00:02:30.710
But I think at this point, hopefully people
are starting to understand it could cost about

00:02:30.710 --> 00:02:32.470
$100 billion a year to

00:02:32.470 --> 00:02:35.730
do the testing that we need to do that we
should be doing.

00:02:35.730 --> 00:02:40.090
But that's way cheaper than the trillions
that's being required in government stimulus

00:02:40.090 --> 00:02:44.110
to try to keep the economy something approximating
afloat.

00:02:44.110 --> 00:02:49.300
So one of the things that you see in this
Morgan Stanley roadmap is that by the end

00:02:49.300 --> 00:02:54.270
of April, we need to be at the testing capacity
of 500000 tests a day.

00:02:54.270 --> 00:02:55.940
Right now, we're at about 20 percent of that.

00:02:55.940 --> 00:02:59.180
We're testing about 100 to 150 thousand a
day.

00:02:59.180 --> 00:03:00.880
Within the next two weeks.

00:03:00.880 --> 00:03:05.800
We need to up that to 500000 tests today by
the end of May.

00:03:05.800 --> 00:03:09.320
We need to be at a million test today and
testing capacity.

00:03:09.320 --> 00:03:10.620
Now, that's conservative.

00:03:10.620 --> 00:03:14.671
I've seen other roadmaps that say we need
22 million tests today because that will let

00:03:14.671 --> 00:03:19.780
you test the entire U.S. population every
two weeks on a rolling basis at minimum.

00:03:19.780 --> 00:03:26.040
Any serious get back to work plan has to be
testing one million per day by June.

00:03:26.040 --> 00:03:29.430
We need what's called serology testing, widely
available.

00:03:29.430 --> 00:03:31.410
These are the antibody tests so that you can

00:03:31.410 --> 00:03:33.170
identify by June.

00:03:33.170 --> 00:03:37.550
It's likely that the real number of people
who who will have had coronavirus in the U.S.

00:03:37.550 --> 00:03:38.910
will be in the millions.

00:03:38.910 --> 00:03:41.250
You want to be able to identify those people.

00:03:41.250 --> 00:03:47.950
And then and if you can do all of these things,
you continue social distancing for a while.

00:03:47.950 --> 00:03:52.000
You get your testing capacity to a million
a day and you get antibody testing widely

00:03:52.000 --> 00:03:55.830
available in mid-June, what we call Wave 1
could go back to work.

00:03:55.830 --> 00:04:00.430
That's the people that were infected early
and potentially some people who are determined

00:04:00.430 --> 00:04:03.560
to be at very, very low risk.

00:04:03.560 --> 00:04:10.700
Wave two of going back to work would be somewhere
around, according to this plan, July 15.

00:04:10.700 --> 00:04:14.700
And then we get to the discussion about schools
and vaccines.

00:04:14.700 --> 00:04:20.970
This roadmap says schools could reopen in
mid-September if everything else happens.

00:04:20.970 --> 00:04:25.040
And then there could be a potential vaccine
for health care workers in late October.

00:04:25.040 --> 00:04:29.440
Now, I know that a lot of people are going
to resist accepting that as a possibility.

00:04:29.440 --> 00:04:31.780
We were told, you know, the vaccine will take
a

00:04:31.780 --> 00:04:33.880
year to a year and a half.

00:04:33.880 --> 00:04:35.160
That is all true.

00:04:35.160 --> 00:04:40.970
But we heard from the lead Corona virus vaccine
research person at the National Institute

00:04:40.970 --> 00:04:41.970
of Health.

00:04:41.970 --> 00:04:45.560
She was on Anderson Cooper's program on Tuesday
night.

00:04:45.560 --> 00:04:49.750
She said the way things are going, if things
go well, we don't need a miracle.

00:04:49.750 --> 00:04:51.970
We just need to avoid setbacks.

00:04:51.970 --> 00:04:56.340
We would likely have a strong enough efficacy
signal from one of the vaccine candidates

00:04:56.340 --> 00:04:59.390
where we could start giving it to health care
workers in.

00:04:59.390 --> 00:05:03.090
Fall under an emergency use protocol.

00:05:03.090 --> 00:05:10.160
The idea being, again, if things go well,
will no setbacks, no miracles.

00:05:10.160 --> 00:05:15.480
The vaccine could start to be broadly available
in March of 2021.

00:05:15.480 --> 00:05:23.840
So the idea here is let's find a less economically
costly way to save lives.

00:05:23.840 --> 00:05:30.890
If you're confident that you are isolating
infected people, those not infected can be

00:05:30.890 --> 00:05:37.050
tested for antibodies and sent back, monitored
for symptoms and tested for the virus.

00:05:37.050 --> 00:05:38.210
This can be done.

00:05:38.210 --> 00:05:39.230
We're not helpless.

00:05:39.230 --> 00:05:42.320
We just don't have the resources we need to
do it every day.

00:05:42.320 --> 00:05:45.319
Trump is asked about testing and he says testing
is great.

00:05:45.319 --> 00:05:46.319
We have the most tests.

00:05:46.319 --> 00:05:47.330
We have the best tests.

00:05:47.330 --> 00:05:49.950
Now, the best tests is a question mark.

00:05:49.950 --> 00:05:52.060
The most tests depends on what you mean.

00:05:52.060 --> 00:05:55.550
We certainly are not doing the most testing
per capita testing per capita.

00:05:55.550 --> 00:05:58.990
We're right around 30, 50 or 40th in the world.

00:05:58.990 --> 00:06:01.490
You have to look at what South Korea was able
to do.

00:06:01.490 --> 00:06:05.480
You have to look at what Singapore did, although
they have a bit bit of a different tolerance.

00:06:05.480 --> 00:06:06.850
These are examples.

00:06:06.850 --> 00:06:10.300
The reemergence risk is very, very big.

00:06:10.300 --> 00:06:16.950
And if we don't do something like what the
Morgan Stanley roadmap suggests and again,

00:06:16.950 --> 00:06:23.230
this is not Morgan Stanley ideas, this is
just Morgan Stanley hiring doctors and economists

00:06:23.230 --> 00:06:25.200
to work on something like this.

00:06:25.200 --> 00:06:31.350
What you can what you don't want to do is
start opening areas and causing spikes.

00:06:31.350 --> 00:06:34.560
And you're going to have like a whack a mole
opening and shutting.

00:06:34.560 --> 00:06:38.810
For the next 18 months, if you do it that
way, that's terrible for lives and it's bad

00:06:38.810 --> 00:06:40.270
for the economy.

00:06:40.270 --> 00:06:45.270
What we need to do is say the hundred billion
a year at all costs to do the testing we need

00:06:45.270 --> 00:06:50.070
to do is cheaper than the stimulus and it's
better for people and it's better for the

00:06:50.070 --> 00:06:51.490
economy.

00:06:51.490 --> 00:06:57.490
Get the serology antibody testing going, start
returning, wave one to work, wave two to work.

00:06:57.490 --> 00:06:59.970
But the testing has to come first.

00:06:59.970 --> 00:07:05.639
And as always, every single one of these steps
is if everything goes well.

00:07:05.639 --> 00:07:12.310
When you've got people in Michigan, more recently
in Kentucky that are ignoring social distancing

00:07:12.310 --> 00:07:14.970
rules, they're going out and protesting.

00:07:14.970 --> 00:07:19.820
They're starting the Don't Tread on me type
stuff about restricted civil liberties and

00:07:19.820 --> 00:07:20.889
on and on and on.

00:07:20.889 --> 00:07:22.290
And there's really no risk here.

00:07:22.290 --> 00:07:27.310
And we've got to open things up with no actual
regard for what's going on.

00:07:27.310 --> 00:07:28.810
Then none of these roadmaps will work.

00:07:28.810 --> 00:07:33.210
The roadmaps assume that everybody plays ball
and that people actually pay attention.

00:07:33.210 --> 00:07:36.930
And the unfortunate reality is only part of
the country is doing that.

00:07:36.930 --> 00:07:45.190
And so my point here is to say you don't have
to sacrifice the economy in total in order

00:07:45.190 --> 00:07:47.260
to preserve health.

00:07:47.260 --> 00:07:52.860
What we're missing that lets us do that, though,
and put a plan together that starts to help

00:07:52.860 --> 00:07:53.860
the economy

00:07:53.860 --> 00:07:58.370
while still keeping people safe as we need
dramatically more testing.

00:07:58.370 --> 00:07:59.510
And it's not.

00:07:59.510 --> 00:08:04.190
We're talking about going from one hundred
twenty five thousand ish tests a day to at

00:08:04.190 --> 00:08:05.630
least a million a day.

00:08:05.630 --> 00:08:08.669
That's going 10x our current capacity.

00:08:08.669 --> 00:08:13.940
And even better would be to get five million
tests a day, 10 million tests today.

00:08:13.940 --> 00:08:18.830
The saliva test may help the Abbott tests.

00:08:18.830 --> 00:08:23.660
They're not really helping very much because
you the test takes five to fifteen minutes,

00:08:23.660 --> 00:08:26.390
but each machine can only do one test at a
time.

00:08:26.390 --> 00:08:30.880
So if they each take five minutes, they can
only do twelve twelve tests an hour.

00:08:30.880 --> 00:08:32.099
They take fifteen minutes.

00:08:32.099 --> 00:08:33.960
They can only do four tests an hour.

00:08:33.960 --> 00:08:38.870
So step testing has to be scaled up and Trump
is pretending everything's fine.

00:08:38.870 --> 00:08:43.140
That is a very serious problem and it's become
politicized.

00:08:43.140 --> 00:08:47.590
Any question about testing during the press
briefings now is seen as a hostile question

00:08:47.590 --> 00:08:53.360
by Trump causing him to just spout the lies
that we have the most and the best tests.

00:08:53.360 --> 00:08:55.430
And we're not gonna get anywhere until that
changes.

00:08:55.430 --> 00:09:00.860
In the meantime, very quickly, we are starting
to see this battle between Donald Trump and

00:09:00.860 --> 00:09:01.860
governors.

00:09:01.860 --> 00:09:04.330
I believe the governors have the constitution
on their side.

00:09:04.330 --> 00:09:09.240
I believe federalism makes it very clear that
Donald Trump does not have the authority to

00:09:09.240 --> 00:09:11.080
force any state to open.

00:09:11.080 --> 00:09:14.660
But that is increasingly a battle that that
is going to be fought.

00:09:14.660 --> 00:09:18.779
Let me know what you think about this general
roadmap.

00:09:18.779 --> 00:09:24.900
This general paradigm for a roadmap to both
keeping people safe, but starting to reopen

00:09:24.900 --> 00:09:26.480
elements of the economy.

00:09:26.480 --> 00:09:29.490
And do you agree that it only works if everybody
plays ball?

00:09:29.490 --> 00:09:32.080
I think that's the most important thing.

00:09:32.080 --> 00:09:37.010
And pretending that some states can do nothing
while other states follow a roadmap.

00:09:37.010 --> 00:09:38.589
It's not going to work friends.

00:09:38.589 --> 00:09:40.270
It's just not going to work.

00:09:40.270 --> 00:09:41.270
Let me know your thoughts.

00:09:41.270 --> 00:09:43.180
I'm on Twitter at David Pakman.

00:09:43.180 --> 00:10:25.040
And we have a fantastic Friday program

00:10:25.040 --> 00:10:33.570
for you today.

00:10:33.570 --> 00:11:01.640
I'm so glad you're joining me.

